#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173078} :activity/id #uuid "96209175-82ce-55f2-a230-37e3aed97893" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049866 :topic/id #uuid "19a3820a-0bf7-5130-9182-e23d96b83145" :topic/math-academy-id 3298} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173020 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173214 :tutorial/content "Suppose we draw two independent random samples of sizes $n_x=10$ and $n_y=15$ from two normally distributed populations $X$ and $Y$ with *unknown* population means $\\mu_x$ and $\\mu_y$ but *known* population variances $\\sigma_x^2=9$ and $\\sigma_y^2=10,$ respectively. \n\nAfter processing our results, we find that the means of these samples are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overline{x}=14, \\qquad\n\\overline{y}=11\n$$\n\n \n\nThese are unbiased point estimates of the corresponding population means $\\mu_x$ and $\\mu_y.$ \n\nNow, by computing the difference \n\n$$\n\\overline{x} - \\overline{y} = 14 - 11 = 3,\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain a point estimate of the difference \n\n$$\n\\mu_x - \\mu_y.\n$$\n\nHowever, instead of reporting a single estimate $\\overline{x}-\\overline{y},$ it's often helpful to give a confidence interval of the form\n\n$$\n\\Big( (\\overline{x}-\\overline{y})-e, \\: (\\overline{x}-\\overline{y})+e \\Big),\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $e$ is some margin of error.\n\nLet's now discuss how to compute a confidence interval in this case." :tutorial/id #uuid "695a9185-0d00-5311-a708-a65e9a62347b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6262 :tutorial/title "Confidence Intervals"} :step/id #uuid "aa9ba62a-69ca-5114-ba83-8de6fe5cf6b2" :step/math-academy-id 37962 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173022}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173021 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172105 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171683 :question/id #uuid "7d75f249-230d-5252-9db1-31f142ed7e70" :question/math-academy-id "e-10062"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "21ac91dd-ea0e-5432-8431-f5fa4d32c6bb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049652} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049812} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050921}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172328} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172374} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500286} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500314} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500326} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500363} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540024} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540099}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals Given Two Normal Populations"} :step/id #uuid "2207172f-f6f6-5eab-b3f5-1e77ec5b503a" :step/math-academy-id 37965 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173071}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173022 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173215 :tutorial/content "In our example, the samples are independent, and the populations are normally distributed. Therefore, the difference of sample means is also normally distributed, where\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} - \\overline{Y} \\sim N\\left( \\mu_x-\\mu_y,  \\dfrac{\\sigma_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{\\sigma_y^2}{n_y}\\right).\n$$\n\nBy transforming $\\overline{X}-\\overline{Y}$ to a standard normal random variable, we have\n\n$$\nZ = \\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\overline{Y}- (\\mu_x - \\mu_y)}{\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sigma_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{\\sigma_y^2}{n_y}}} \\sim N(0,1).\n$$\n\nWe wish to find a $z$-interval that we're $95\\%$ confident that the random variable $Z$ lies within. This interval is indicated in the diagram below:\n\n![](images/50/50a0f49fe5c96ba3259832fbf1a0abcedffb342301ca069bb7b88d157a181019.png)\n\n  \n\nTo help with the notation, we define a new parameter $\\alpha.$ Since we're computing a $95\\%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.95=0.05\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ is precisely the area bounded by each \"tail\" that we're excluding from our confidence interval. Let's label the critical values at the endpoints of our interval as $\\pm z_{\\alpha/2}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/3c/3cdfd2c72bf7fe65e5d1b584d46d4c846e74615e54c739a6d1ea990c95011a8a.png)\n\n  \n\nAccording to our diagram,\n\n$$\nP(Z > z_{0.025}) = P(Z < -z_{0.025}) = 0.025.\n$$\n\n  \n\nUsing a percentage points table for the standard normal distribution, we find that $z_{0.025} \\approx 1.96.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(Z > 1.96) = P(Z < -1.96) = 0.025.\n$$\n\nIn other words,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-1.96<Z<1.96)=0.95.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, there is a $95\\%$ probability that our random variable $Z$ will lie in the interval $(-1.96, 1.96).$\n\nLet's consider the inequality inside the parentheses of the last probability statement: \n\n$$\n-1.96 < Z < 1.96\n$$\n\nWriting this in terms of our original variables, we have:\n\n$$\n-1.96 < \\:\\dfrac{\\overline{x} - \\overline{y} - (\\mu_x - \\mu_y)}{\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sigma_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{\\sigma_y^2}{n_y}}} < 1.96\n$$\n\nSolving this for the difference $\\mu_x-\\mu_y,$ we get \n\n$$\n(\\overline{x} - \\overline{y}) - 1.96 \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sigma_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{\\sigma_y^2}{n_y}} < \\: \\mu_x - \\mu_y < (\\overline{x} - \\overline{y}) + 1.96 \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sigma_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{\\sigma_y^2}{n_y}} .\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the difference $\\mu_x - \\mu_y$ of population means is given by\n\n$$\n\\left((\\overline{x} - \\overline{y}) - 1.96 \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sigma_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{\\sigma_y^2}{n_y}},\\: (\\overline{x} - \\overline{y}) + 1.96 \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sigma_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{\\sigma_y^2}{n_y}} \\: \\right).\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting our values\n\n$$\nn_x=10, \\quad\nn_y=15, \\quad\n\\overline{x}=14, \\quad\n\\overline{y}=11, \\quad\n\\sigma_x^2=9, \\quad \n\\sigma_y^2=10,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that our $95\\%$ confidence interval for $\\mu_x-\\mu_y$ is \n\n$$\n\\big( 3 - 2.453, \\: 3 + 2.453\\big).\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's now discuss the general procedure." :tutorial/id #uuid "704df253-ee0a-5b3d-b203-091cffab5ef7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6263 :tutorial/title "Constructing Confidence Intervals"} :step/id #uuid "ec9a1081-46c6-531a-80ec-11779a390dc8" :step/math-academy-id 37967 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173070}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173023 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172106 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171684 :question/id #uuid "b8eff294-354a-5622-acfe-ca148de26225" :question/math-academy-id "e-10063"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "21507a23-f729-56aa-8e9c-66eb5823a2d8" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172329} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500316} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500340} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500352} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500359} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510417} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540111}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals Given Two Large Samples"} :step/id #uuid "b67d5d70-f31f-5e3f-863b-0253f887c7b7" :step/math-academy-id 37968 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173024}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173024 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173216 :tutorial/content "A confidence interval for the difference $\\mu_x-\\mu_y$ between the population means can be interpreted in the following ways:\n\n- If all values within the confidence interval are *positive*, this suggests that $\\mu_x - \\mu_y > 0.$ In other words \n\n$$\n\\mu_x > \\mu_y.\n$$\n\nWe should carry out a statistical hypothesis test to formally verify this claim.\n\n- If all values within the confidence interval are *negative*, this suggests that $\\mu_x - \\mu_y < 0.$ In other words \n\n$$\n\\mu_x < \\mu_y.\n$$\n\nAgain, we should conduct a statistical hypothesis test to verify this claim.\n\n- If the confidence interval contains zero, then there is *insufficient evidence* of a statistically significant difference between the two means. Therefore, we conclude\n\n$$\n\\mu_x \\approx \\mu_y.\n$$\n\nLet's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "91974784-1cc3-5ee0-8779-aa6f11e869b1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6264 :tutorial/title "Interpreting Confidence Intervals"} :step/id #uuid "431f12d4-556e-5091-b97c-8c38fce53091" :step/math-academy-id 37970 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173067}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173067 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172129 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171709 :question/id #uuid "ce645810-49c8-5b0c-8fb4-934f3b65f900" :question/math-academy-id "e-11111"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "be6d2ce2-f7cc-5a87-86d1-21f769dbd580" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172369} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500301} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500344} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500368} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510397} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510398} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510430} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540042} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540051}] :knowledge-point/title "Applications of Confidence Intervals"} :step/id #uuid "540635d6-7f7f-5be7-942e-138c45baf88b" :step/math-academy-id 42221} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173070 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173242 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have independent random samples of sizes $n_x$ and $n_y$ from normal populations with unknown population means $\\mu_x$ and $\\mu_y$ and *known* population variances $\\sigma_x^2$ and $\\sigma_y^2.$ \n\nFor a given value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ a $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the difference $\\mu_x - \\mu_y$ between the population means is given by\n\n$$\n\\left((\\overline{x}-\\overline{y}) - z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sigma_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{\\sigma_y^2}{n_y}}, \\: (\\overline{x}-\\overline{y}) + z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sigma_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{\\sigma_y^2}{n_y}} \\: \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2},$ and $Z \\sim N(0,1).$ \n\nThe confidence limits (i.e., endpoints of our interval) are given by\n\n$$\n(\\overline{x}-\\overline{y}) \\: \\pm \\: z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sigma_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{\\sigma_y^2}{n_y}}.\n$$\n\nAs usual, each part of the formula above has a name:\n\n- $\\overline{x}-\\overline{y}$ is an estimate of the difference of the population means\n\n- $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sigma_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{\\sigma_y^2}{n_y}}$ is the standard error\n\n- $z_{\\alpha/2}$ is the corresponding $z$-score\n\n- $E = z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sigma_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{\\sigma_y^2}{n_y}}$ is the margin of error\n\n![](images/67/6711538f5dd66f698fd39bc9e2f6e4e6be86e1a5b4d7392ec097822c67328466.svg)\n\nSo, our confidence interval can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}((\\overset{―}{x}-\\overset{―}{y})-[\\text{margin of error}], & \\,(\\overset{―}{x}-\\overset{―}{y})+[\\text{margin of error}]) \\\\ ((\\overset{―}{x}-\\overset{―}{y})-[\\text{[math]z[/math]-score}]\\cdot [\\text{standard error}], & \\,(\\overset{―}{x}-\\overset{―}{y})+[\\text{[math]z[/math]-score}]\\cdot [\\text{standard error}]) \\\\ ((\\overset{―}{x}-\\overset{―}{y})-{z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{x}^{2}}{{n}_{x}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{y}^{2}}{{n}_{y}}}, & \\,(\\overset{―}{x}-\\overset{―}{y})+{z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{x}^{2}}{{n}_{x}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{y}^{2}}{{n}_{y}}}\\,)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince computing a confidence interval amounts to finding the corresponding confidence limits, we will use the two terms interchangeably." :tutorial/id #uuid "ebbe2542-de1d-5e09-a2f6-333861a0528b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6993 :tutorial/title "A Summary"} :step/id #uuid "ad1c19f4-1559-53d1-9794-e458c1c2bf44" :step/math-academy-id 42239 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173021}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173071 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173243 :tutorial/content "Up to now, we've assumed that the underlying populations are normally distributed. However, if both samples are *sufficiently large,* then according to the central limit theorem, we can use the approximation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\overline{Y}- (\\mu_x - \\mu_y)}{\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sigma_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{\\sigma_y^2}{n_y}}}  \\approx N(0,1).\n$$\n\nTypically, \"sufficiently large\" means that $n_x \\geq 30$ *and* $n_y\\geq 30.$\n\nSo, even though we might not know the underlying distribution, we can still use the following formula for the confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n(\\overline{x}-\\overline{y}) \\: \\pm \\: z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sigma_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{\\sigma_y^2}{n_y}}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2e81e4a9-e1da-5d2a-a442-d37e60bb4156" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6994 :tutorial/title "Large Samples"} :step/id #uuid "1c508e7a-641a-5325-83a8-743ec1e87cc8" :step/math-academy-id 42240 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173023}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173078 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173250 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we'll learn how to construct confidence intervals for the difference between two population means in cases where the population variances are known.\n\nThroughout this lesson, we assume that samples from two groups, $X$ and $Y,$ are **independent.** Two groups are independent if they are not directly related or connected. Specifically:\n\n- The selection or outcome of one group does not influence the selection or outcome of the other.\n\n- The individuals or data points within each group are not paired or matched in any way, and there is no inherent relationship between the observations in the two groups.\n\nFor example, suppose an educational institution wants to test the effectiveness of a new exam format. The institution believes that students perform better under the new format. To assess this belief, they could conduct independent or dependent samples. Let's discuss an example of each.\n\n**Independent Samples**\n\nThe institution randomly selects 100 students, 50 of whom are randomly assigned to sit the exam under the new format (the experimental group), and 50 to take the exam under the traditional format (the control group). Both groups sit their respective exams, and the results are compared.\n\nIn this case, the experimental and control groups represent independent samples, as the outcomes in one group are not influenced by the outcomes in the other group, and the students are not paired or matched in any way.\n\n**Dependent Samples**\n\nThe institution randomly selects 50 students. First, each student sits the exam under the traditional format, and the results are recorded (baseline measurement). Then, all students take the exam under the new format, and their performance is measured again (post-experiment measurement).\n\nIn this case, the two groups (baseline and post-experiment measurements) represent dependent samples. This is because the data is collected from the same set of students, and each student's post-experiment performance is related to their baseline performance." :tutorial/id #uuid "63b6e9f6-3488-5ee7-a2bb-fe53e6215766" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7011 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "72956012-9353-5025-9918-764ecdd18913" :step/math-academy-id 42313 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173079}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173079 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173251 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have two normally distributed populations $X$ and $Y,$ where\n\n$$\nX\\sim N(\\mu_x, \\sigma^2_x), \\qquad Y\\sim N(\\mu_y, \\sigma^2_y).\n$$\n\n \n\nIf we conduct a random sample of size $n_x$ from the first population and a second, *independent* random sample of size $n_y$ from the second population, then we know that\n\n$$\n\\overline{X}\\sim N\\left(\\mu_x,\\dfrac{\\sigma_x^2}{n_x}\\right), \\qquad \\overline{Y}\\sim N\\left(\\mu_y,\\dfrac{\\sigma_y^2}{n_y}\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overline{X}$ and $\\overline{Y}$ are the sample means. \n\nNow, we know that the sum or difference of two independent normally distributed random variables is also normal. Moreover, using our usual results for subtracting means and variances, we have\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} - \\overline{Y} \\sim N\\left(\\mu_x - \\mu_y,\\dfrac{\\sigma_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{\\sigma_y^2}{n_y} \\right).\n$$\n\nThis means that the random variable $Z,$ defined as\n\n$$\nZ =\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\overline{Y}- (\\mu_x - \\mu_y)}{\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sigma_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{\\sigma_y^2}{n_y}}}\n$$\n\nfollows a standard normal distribution $N(0,1).$\n\nLet's look at a concrete example that uses this result to construct a confidence interval." :tutorial/id #uuid "4ffb5d6a-726b-5d7e-a87e-58ad57ff2639" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7012 :tutorial/title "Sampling Distributions of the Difference of Two Means"} :step/id #uuid "0b19ba07-fb9f-5fb9-a67d-7b923e9a1e35" :step/math-academy-id 42314 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173020}}] :activity/title "Confidence Intervals for Two Means: Known and Unequal Population Variances" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186172147}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510502} :activity/id #uuid "edcee70a-6434-5e86-b8e7-971648d37f3e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049866 :topic/id #uuid "19a3820a-0bf7-5130-9182-e23d96b83145" :topic/math-academy-id 3298} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510501 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510397 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fea37df-e66f-522e-b62b-c4201b1b4816" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509719} {:answer/id #uuid "23e3d9b1-9b71-54ad-a4bf-163a871b7d0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509765} {:answer/id #uuid "62f9b791-9810-563a-bab9-7a82307110c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509894} {:answer/id #uuid "91ed605b-90ba-53ea-9dfb-1121054ab8be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509998} {:answer/id #uuid "dd43de8d-951e-5173-a7ee-0e8b7890925f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510159}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509998} :answer-field/id #uuid "bcbeeea1-2823-5cdd-8385-469893fd88bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510335}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "5acbf13f-ff67-5449-9947-a8679cb075b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-159664" :question/problem "The scores of girls and boys on a national post-high school exam have standard deviations of $3$ points and $4$ points, respectively. The authorities want to determine whether the population mean ${\\mu }_{x}$ of the girls' scores is the same as the population mean ${\\mu }_{y}$ of the boys' scores. They construct a $95$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means.\n\nOfficials sampled $111$ girls and $115$ boys and found that their mean scores on the test were $74$ points and $73.4$ points, respectively. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The end-points of the $95%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means are approximately $0.6\\pm 0.92.$\n\nII. All values contained within the $95%$ confidence interval are positive.\n\nIII. The confidence interval data suggests that ${\\mu }_{x}>{\\mu }_{y}.$\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both sample sizes are \"sufficiently large,\" i.e., $\\ge 30,$ and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=74-73.6=0.6\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.025}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.025})=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.025}=1.960:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\n \n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.960\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{3}^{2}}{111}+\\frac{{4}^{2}}{115}}≈0.920\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.6\\pm 0.920\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The end-points of our confidence interval are $0.6\\pm 0.920.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The confidence interval contains $0:$\n\n$$\n(0.6-0.92,\\,0.6+0.92)=(-0.32,\\,1.52)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the confidence interval contains zero, it remains possible that ${\\mu }_{x}={\\mu }_{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :step/id #uuid "517bedd8-acc9-5598-9606-561a1e7bcb04" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510560}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510502 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510417 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "016ded86-d025-503c-b985-6d6426861714" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11\\pm 0.701" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509697} {:answer/id #uuid "068bb060-b53b-584e-bc05-e9b1925f5bf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11\\pm 0.747" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509705} {:answer/id #uuid "37885ebf-04e6-5d47-8e02-92e0f6c71755" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11\\pm 1.344" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509808} {:answer/id #uuid "b5897ae1-26f4-5f76-a208-ed3f569c0dfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11\\pm 1.649" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510078} {:answer/id #uuid "e7086e67-8297-5052-9066-130384411f8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11\\pm 1.433" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510178}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509808} :answer-field/id #uuid "05a48ccb-a74e-541e-97fa-2294603b02e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510242}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7f9f9f92-90ae-5c14-9ede-c5718a2ba70e" :question/math-academy-id "q-159628" :question/problem "Consider a sample of size ${n}_{1}=54$ from a population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{1}=3.7$ and a sample of size ${n}_{2}=77$ from a population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{2}=1.2.$ Given that the samples are independent and the sample means are ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}=23$ and  ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=12,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both sample sizes are \"sufficiently large,\" i.e., $\\ge 30,$ and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=23-12=11\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.005}=2.576.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=2.576\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{3.7}^{2}}{54}+\\frac{{1.2}^{2}}{77}}≈1.344\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $99%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 11\\pm 1.344\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5207b2ac-ba48-5823-9d3d-fc02b606d266" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510564}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510529 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510430 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "191026ef-026f-56e1-aed9-51375e82a905" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509738} {:answer/id #uuid "39af70a3-b7c9-5157-a843-61baeb776152" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509812} {:answer/id #uuid "86b3b730-b022-5e9e-ae72-0ac285e66745" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509981} {:answer/id #uuid "adadc4f6-e872-59e0-a4f8-ce8c2ef02108" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510061} {:answer/id #uuid "e2f6975c-69e9-5183-8d49-09c3be9f6789" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510171}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509738} :answer-field/id #uuid "5eb63ba3-c644-5698-927f-01d281fe6523" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510288}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a0faeac4-bc51-5e0f-965c-b834d48291a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-159636" :question/problem "The lengths of traditional baguettes and brown baguettes made at a particular French bakery have standard deviations of $4\\,\\text{cm}$ and $3\\,\\text{cm},$ respectively. The baker wants to determine whether the population mean ${\\mu }_{x}$ of the lengths of traditional baguettes is larger than the population mean ${\\mu }_{y}$ of the lengths of brown baguettes. He constructs a $90%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means.\n\nGiven that the baker sampled $50$ traditional baguettes and $40$ brown baguettes and found that their mean lengths were $65\\,\\text{cm}$ and $63.5\\,\\text{cm},$ respectively, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The end-points of the $90%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means are approximately $1.5\\pm 1.421.$\n\nII. All values contained within the $90%$ confidence interval are positive.\n\nIII. The confidence interval data suggests that ${\\mu }_{x}>{\\mu }_{y}.$\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both sample sizes are \"sufficiently large,\" i.e., $\\ge 30,$ and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=65-63.5=1.5\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.90=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.05}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.05})=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.05}=1.645:$  \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\n \n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.645\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{4}^{2}}{50}+\\frac{{3}^{2}}{40}}≈1.214\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 1.5\\pm 1.214\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The end-points of our confidence interval are $1.5\\pm 1.214.$\n\n- Statement II is true. All values within the confidence interval are positive:\n\n$$\n(1.5-1.214,\\,1.5+1.214)=(0.286,\\,2.714)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since all values within the confidence interval are positive, this suggests that ${\\mu }_{x}>{\\mu }_{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "a2ed7b82-21f1-5f47-b31c-6616cabb005c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510501}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510560 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510398 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "341e9984-edd6-50e2-bcfc-5f3f669d286d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509798} {:answer/id #uuid "41f44e83-0d9c-596d-b75d-78d997dc828e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509827} {:answer/id #uuid "a7a8c787-0565-563f-b6ab-1b60b46da41c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510041} {:answer/id #uuid "bda6715a-bd7f-5810-9508-0c32ee7cc877" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510088} {:answer/id #uuid "f5bc53a9-6a52-56d2-9347-e34017750f74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510213}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509798} :answer-field/id #uuid "63a3ec7b-79e1-5862-a616-14f2961b8754" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510293}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "5b6883ab-506f-535a-b78d-aa1454cc2ffc" :question/math-academy-id "q-159667" :question/problem "A study of body weight conducted on two age groups of women, X and Y, yields standard deviations of ${\\sigma }_{x}=8\\,\\text{lb}$ and ${\\sigma }_{y}=10\\,\\text{lb},$ respectively.\nA researcher wants to determine whether the population mean ${\\mu }_{x}$ of the weights of group X is smaller than the population mean ${\\mu }_{y}$ of the weights of group Y. She decides to construct a $90%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means.\n\nThe researcher sampled $50$ women from group X and $55$ from group Y, finding that their mean weights are $58\\,lb$ and $64\\,lb,$  respectively. Which of the following statements are true?  \n\nI. The end-points of the $90%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means are approximately $-6\\pm 2.895.$\n\nII. All values contained within the $90%$ confidence interval are negative.\n\nIII. The confidence interval data suggests that ${\\mu }_{x}<{\\mu }_{y}.$\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both sample sizes are \"sufficiently large,\" i.e., $\\ge 30,$ and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=58-64=-6\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.90=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.05}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.05})=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.05}=1.645:$  \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\n \n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.645\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{8}^{2}}{50}+\\frac{{10}^{2}}{55}}≈2.895\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -6\\pm 2.895\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The end-points of our confidence interval are $-6\\pm 2.895.$\n\n- Statement II is true. All values within the confidence interval are negative:\n\n$$\n(-6-2.895,\\,-6+2.895)=(-8.895,\\,-3.105)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since all values within the confidence interval are negative, this suggests that ${\\mu }_{x}<{\\mu }_{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} :step/id #uuid "eeaf510f-334c-5379-92ee-488a7974c6bf"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510564 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510379 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ff10644-8d80-59d2-847c-acdc62e1d31c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7\\pm 1.403" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509856} {:answer/id #uuid "5737153b-7255-54c5-9ed1-76b4f29f597d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7\\pm 1.837" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509869} {:answer/id #uuid "61672d44-76c1-5941-96f9-b547c24560e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7\\pm 1.304" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509890} {:answer/id #uuid "b148fa07-0393-5190-a3cf-99f1bae95ad4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7\\pm 1.034" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510070} {:answer/id #uuid "f8b14202-3b4b-5788-bb0b-55e0f3678b90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7\\pm 0.587" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510218}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509890} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e4d1d6d-2c26-56bc-a27d-991d70114cf3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510287}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "33a01272-f3c9-5c47-b349-cebcb033572c" :question/math-academy-id "q-159630" :question/problem "Consider a sample of size ${n}_{1}=82$ from a population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{1}=4.6$ and a sample of size ${n}_{2}=73$ from a population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{2}=5.2.$ Given that the samples are independent and the sample means are ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}=33$ and  ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=26,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both sample sizes are \"sufficiently large,\" i.e., $\\ge 30,$ and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=33-26=7\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.05}=1.645.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.645\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{4.6}^{2}}{82}+\\frac{{5.2}^{2}}{73}}≈1.304\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $90%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 7\\pm 1.304\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f9b44149-b78f-586f-aef5-56e50d096b88" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510529}}] :activity/title "Confidence Intervals for Two Means: Known and Unequal Population Variances" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509691}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540137} :activity/id #uuid "7e10f26f-73c5-59a7-bac0-7cdb8bdd4de0" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049866 :topic/id #uuid "19a3820a-0bf7-5130-9182-e23d96b83145" :topic/math-academy-id 3298} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540137 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540051 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20a47bcb-1ece-5126-b658-82da8b2c4b4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539544} {:answer/id #uuid "5ffb1421-5374-56b5-a2bb-9fb21023bbd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539640} {:answer/id #uuid "e32bf06a-c78d-5f2e-a3f1-fa589a9d0e2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539867} {:answer/id #uuid "f66c0a76-54b6-5957-a194-faca4954715b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539892} {:answer/id #uuid "f6f2e26f-5082-5bf5-b032-5423339dd37a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539893}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539867} :answer-field/id #uuid "5298ca4d-ecde-5661-bd4e-59d6462c363f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539956}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "5391d4bc-fe22-5a3b-85bc-6502aec7782e" :question/math-academy-id "q-159626" :question/problem "The weights of the wooden and steel chairs produced by a furniture company have standard deviations of $2\\,\\text{lb}$ and $1\\,\\text{lb},$ respectively. The company wants to determine whether the population mean ${\\mu }_{x}$ of weights for the wooden chairs is greater than the population mean ${\\mu }_{y}$ of weights for the steel chairs. They construct a $95%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means.\n\nGiven that the company sampled $100$ wooden chairs and $150$ steel chairs and found that their mean weights are $10\\,\\text{lb}$ and $9\\,\\text{lb},$ respectively, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The end-points of the $95%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means are approximately $1\\pm 0.423.$\n\nII. All values contained within the $95%$ confidence interval are positive.\n\nIII. The confidence interval data suggests that ${\\mu }_{x}>{\\mu }_{y}.$\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" :question/worked-solution "Notice that \n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both sample sizes are \"sufficiently large,\" i.e., $\\ge 30,$ and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=10-9=1\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.025}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.025})=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.025}=1.960:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.960\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{2}^{2}}{100}+\\frac{{1}^{2}}{150}}≈0.423\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 1\\pm 0.423\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The end-points of our confidence interval are $1\\pm 0.423.$\n\n- Statement II is true. All values within the confidence interval are positive:\n\n$$\n(1-0.423,\\,1+0.423)=(0.577,\\,1.423)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since all values within the confidence interval are positive, this suggests that ${\\mu }_{x}>{\\mu }_{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} :step/id #uuid "4d34c056-1894-58b9-beb3-f82d4263465e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540170}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540145 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540042 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65f2c1af-4f10-592c-9fcf-8c3faa46018b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539648} {:answer/id #uuid "73cbb3cd-94a4-531b-8992-a4d90c29793d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539672} {:answer/id #uuid "a14ed575-9de2-5d57-8f0a-151dcd5cb36c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539749} {:answer/id #uuid "d94056d5-23c1-5895-8352-362b6172cbef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539854} {:answer/id #uuid "f83320d5-06d8-528d-a2fa-782e2f50780c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539899}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539854} :answer-field/id #uuid "f24e3ec2-08cb-5869-8ee2-41217f338f4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540014}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "34a9b0ce-4c8f-5634-aaa8-208faeda7581" :question/math-academy-id "q-159641" :question/problem "It is known that the thickness of ceramic tiles from suppliers X and Y are normally distributed with standard deviations of ${\\sigma }_{x}=1.6\\,\\text{mm}$ and ${\\sigma }_{y}=1.2\\,\\text{mm},$ respectively. A construction contractor wants to determine whether the population mean ${\\mu }_{x}$ of the thickness of ceramic tiles from supplier X is greater than the population mean ${\\mu }_{y}$ of the thickness of ceramic tiles from supplier Y. He constructs a $95%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means.\n\nGiven that the contractor sampled $20$ tiles from supplier X and $18$ tiles from supplier Y and found that their mean thicknesses are $18.8\\,\\text{mm}$ and $18.5\\,\\text{mm},$ respectively, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The end-points of the $95%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means are approximately $0.3\\pm 0.894.$\n\nII. All values contained within the $95%$ confidence interval are positive.\n\nIII. The confidence interval data suggests that ${\\mu }_{x}>{\\mu }_{y}.$\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both populations are normally distributed, and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=18.8-18.5=0.3\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.025}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.025})=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.025}=1.960:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.960\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{1.6}^{2}}{20}+\\frac{{1.2}^{2}}{18}}≈0.894\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.3\\pm 0.894\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The end-points of our confidence interval are $0.3\\pm 0.894.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The confidence interval contains $0:$\n\n$$\n(0.3-0.894,\\,0.3+0.894)=(-0.594,\\,1.194)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the confidence interval contains zero, it remains possible that ${\\mu }_{x}={\\mu }_{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :step/id #uuid "6a0cf1ad-754a-5ce6-b1ab-f3b527efb4e9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540152}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540152 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540024 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bb24e5f-7d8c-5eb1-90c8-8d9b94a92255" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7\\pm 3.760" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539558} {:answer/id #uuid "9dba8f44-3248-5a69-9eee-19fc5e572be6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\pm 6.784" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539739} {:answer/id #uuid "b29e678d-3c58-5275-b969-0ca48fcef8d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7\\pm 4.580" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539785} {:answer/id #uuid "b62c0cb8-6799-5ac4-bb8e-3e9824262e66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\pm 4.530" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539797} {:answer/id #uuid "ec4dc5f0-036a-5939-ad15-8aa21a1aa5f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\pm 6.380" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539877}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539877} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe7bf0ba-27c6-5e85-90fc-d44843ac230f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540021}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "12431c38-5ac6-59aa-ba15-2add6471e563" :question/math-academy-id "q-159642" :question/problem "![](images/64/6486d3914bb0f7b4898220512e96630d13d2b0f4a5c1851938f98371a4c0255f.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size ${n}_{1}=30$ from a normal population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{1}=7$ and a sample of size ${n}_{2}=32$ from a normal population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{2}=12.$ Given that the samples are independent and the sample means are ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}=22$ and  ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=16,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both populations are normally distributed, and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=22-16=6\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.005}=2.576.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=2.576\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{7}^{2}}{30}+\\frac{{12}^{2}}{32}}≈6.380\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $99%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 6\\pm 6.380\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7ab50149-598b-562d-b856-92986b35c2f9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540179}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540170 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540111 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4afb2e10-ecca-5296-b23a-fa3c6ff59dac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9\\pm 0.666" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539612} {:answer/id #uuid "b131442b-fa8e-59c6-a34b-54dfaa572ded" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9\\pm 1.911" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539779} {:answer/id #uuid "b4633134-7243-5e28-9de0-0f5355178cf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9\\pm 1.563" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539789} {:answer/id #uuid "e593264d-28ab-56a2-a3df-c5c95f2634fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9\\pm 1.356" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539869} {:answer/id #uuid "fcbef54b-4be0-5d86-b67e-1f0fa388f6ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9\\pm 0.938" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539908}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539869} :answer-field/id #uuid "21552841-7d86-5c0f-9131-48f4014f0900" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539932}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f9f98bfe-eda2-5f27-b0c5-b96f1c4792b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-159634" :question/problem "Consider a sample of size ${n}_{1}=58$ from a population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{1}=3.4$ and a sample of size ${n}_{2}=86$ from a population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{2}=4.9.$ Given that the samples are independent and the sample means are ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}=34$ and  ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=43,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both sample sizes are \"sufficiently large,\" i.e., $\\ge 30,$ and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=34-43=-9\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.025}=1.96.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.96\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{3.4}^{2}}{58}+\\frac{{4.9}^{2}}{86}}≈1.356\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $95%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -9\\pm 1.356\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "dd2b42e2-2329-557d-a4d3-625279cbdfe8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540145}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540179 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540099 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e34c82e-f3f4-523a-ab51-ab809db9fcc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\pm 3.238" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539591} {:answer/id #uuid "6e3f9768-135a-5874-97de-788770ddf0c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\pm 3.118" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539663} {:answer/id #uuid "99447647-4006-52bb-adcd-1816c068b843" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\pm 2.832" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539733} {:answer/id #uuid "9eb298de-a5f9-5a47-b8be-adf5a2151ff2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\pm 3.442" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539742} {:answer/id #uuid "f4288d34-1117-5ed0-b243-2c2c810cc589" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\pm 2.768" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539889}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539663} :answer-field/id #uuid "736c5de7-2fbd-5a65-8262-e8bce4536075" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539964}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "db9cf9a1-6584-5405-8a36-efa72137f427" :question/math-academy-id "q-159643" :question/problem "![](images/69/693576483310a4656435ea4a3d8d9652b8de563d26a87c7c80fb902a3bef1e29.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size ${n}_{1}=32$ from a normal population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{1}=5$ and a sample of size ${n}_{2}=28$ from a normal population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{2}=7.$ Given that the samples are independent and the sample means are ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}=19$ and  ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=11,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both populations are normally distributed, and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=19-11=8\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.025}=1.96.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.96\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{5}^{2}}{32}+\\frac{{7}^{2}}{28}}≈3.118\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $95%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 8\\pm 3.118\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ef47f619-bd0e-5e18-af22-1dd3d52f0247"}] :activity/title "Confidence Intervals for Two Means: Known and Unequal Population Variances" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539472}]}
